.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_a_cmplx (double a_cmplx)"
Subswitch of a_cmplx\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
a_cmplx - Should be an upper limit for the operator complexity which is defined as the ratio between the total number of matrix entries (summed over all AMG levels) and the number of entries in the given matrix (= nna)\&. Depending on the problem and the strategy chosen, this may be a value as low as 1\&.2, but it may also be as high as 4\&.0, say\&. Typical values are 1\&.5-3\&.0\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_chktol (double chktol)"
Checking of input matrix
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Used to control the amount of checking of the input matrix\&.

.PP
<0\&.0d0 No checking\&. This is the standard for production runs\&. =0\&.0d0 Standard checking for logical correctness\&. >0\&.0d0 Enhanced checking\&. The concrete value of chktol serves as a tolerance\&. Standard value: 1\&.0d-7\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_eps (double eps)"
Stopping criterion\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
= 0\&.0 Stopping criteria based in eps is deactivated (only round-off error) 
.PP
.RS 4
0\&.0 Iteration stops if res <= eps\&.res0 (res0 = starting residual) < 0\&.0 Iteration stops if res <= |eps| 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_filnam_dump (const std::string & filnam_dump)"
Switch filnam_dump
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Character\&. File root name which SAMG tries to find for reading\&. If idmp>1, the matrices as described above are dumped to files using the root name filnam_dump, the default name being ‘level’\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_g_cmplx (double g_cmplx)"
Subswitch of g_cmplx\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
g_cmplx - Should be an upper limit for the grid complexity which is defined as the ratio between the total number of variables (summed over all AMG levels) and the number of variables in the given problem (= nnu)\&. Depending on the problem and the strategy chosen, this may be a value as low as 1\&.2\&. Usually, an upper limit is 2\&.0\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iadp (int32_t iadp)"
Subswitch of idump\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Selects print output regarding the coarse-level matrices\&.

.PP
[3:3], iadp >1 is only relevant for coupled systems\&. 0 No particular output\&. 1 Display table on coarse-level matrices (full problem)\&. 2 In addition: same info for all submatrices\&. 3 In addition: connectivity info between unknowns\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_idmp (int32_t idmp)"
Subswitch of idump\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1], idmp 0 Coarsening history 1 Standard print output (coarsening history)\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iextent (int32_t iextent)"
Subswitch of iswitch\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[2:2],iextent Memory extension switch\&. Selects beahaviour when limits of initial dimensioning have been reached

.PP
0 SAMG returns with error code 1 SAMG allocates extra memory and continues (if no core space, writes prev\&. allocated data to disk 2 SAMG allocates ext\&. memory and continues (if no core space, SAMG terminates) 3 SAMG allocates ext\&. memory and continues (prev\&. allocated data is written to disk) 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_igam (int32_t igam)"
Subswitch of ncyc\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1],igam 1 V-cycle (standard) 2 F-cycle 3 W-cycle 4 WW-cycle (very expensive) 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_igdp (int32_t igdp)"
Subswitch of idump\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Selects print output regarding the coarse levels\&.

.PP
[2:2], igdp >1 is only relevant for coupled systems\&. Otherwise: ignored\&. 0 No particular output\&. 1 Display table on grids (full problem)\&. 2 Same for all submatrices (only if nsys>1)\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_internal (int32_t internal)"
Subswitch of nsolve\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[3:3],internal = 0 primary matrix P is user-defined (nprim has to be !0) > 0 P is defined internally 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_ioform (std::string ioform)"
Switch ioform
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Reading or writing can be formatted, unformatted or binary\&. In case of reading, SAMG knows the format by looking into a special “format” file (suffix “\&.frm”, → Section 7\&.8\&.2)\&. In case of writing (→Sections 7\&.8\&.3‐7\&.8\&.5), the hidden char*1 variable ioform defines the format SAMG is supposed to use\&. That is, with the char*1 variable ch being ‘f’, ‘u’, or ‘b’, issue "call samg_set_ioform(ch,1)" to specify the requested format, the default being formatted\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_ioscratch (int32_t ioscratch)"
Subswitch of iswitch\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[6:7],ioscratch Unit number for scratch files used by SAMG for memory management 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iout1 (int32_t iout1)"
Subswitch of iout\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1], iout1, 1 Table of input data and work statistics\&. 2 Standard history of cycling process\&. 3 Extended history: including all levels, full smoothing steps\&. 4 Extended history: including all levels and even partial smoothing steps\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iout2 (int32_t iout2)"
Subswitch of iout\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[2:2], iout2 0 No action\&. 1 Display most relevant SAMG hidden parameters\&. 2 Display all SAMG hidden parameters\&. 3 Display all SAMG hidden parameters in a single list\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_irow (int32_t irow)"
Subswitch of matrix\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[2:2], irow0 1 A is a zero rowsum matrix\&. (For such matrices, the solution will be normalized) 2 A is not a zero rowsum matrix 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iswit (int32_t iswit)"
Subswitch of iswitch\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1], iswit Controls re-use of SAMG decompositions during repeated calls\&. 
.PP
.nf
      5   Complete SAMG run\&. Upon return, memory is released
      4   Same as 5 except memory not released
      3   partial setup: Re-use coarser grids and interpolation but update
          Galerkin operators\&. Memory not released
      2   No setup: Re-use coarser grids, interp\&. and Galerkin from prev\&. run
      1   Same as 2 except SAMG assumes matrix A to be the same as prev\&. run

.fi
.PP

.PP
With settings iswit(4 ,\&.\&.\&., 1), it is the user’s responsibility to employ some kind of “outer” convergence control\&. Such a control is done automatically if the primary solver control is activated\&. This is done by just using the settings iswit( 6,\&.\&.\&., 9) instead of iswit(4 ,\&.\&.\&., 1)\&. More precisely, in solving a series of linear systems, the primary control process starts automatically whenever some iswit(>5) is used, and it will terminate as soon as some iswit(≤ 5) is used\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_isym (int32_t isym)"
Subswitch of matrix\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1], isym 1 A is symmetric 2 A is not symmetric 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iter_pre (int32_t iter_pre)"
Subswich of ncg\&. Sets the hidden parameter iter_pre\&. If iter_pre > 0, SAMG will be used as a pre-conditioner for the BI-CGStab solution\&. A maximum of 2 SAMG pre- condition calls is possible, i\&.e\&. 0 <= iter_pre <= 2; 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_itypu (int32_t itypu)"
Subswitch of ifirst\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1], itypu 0 Actual content of u is chosen as first approximation 1 First approximation u==0 2 First approximation u==1 3 First approximation is a random function

.PP
If itypu == 0, a different convergence criterion should be set based on the norm of the right-hand side vector:

.PP
eps = ||b|| * rel_eps

.PP
rel_eps can be accessed with the function Get_rel_eps 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_iwdp (int32_t iwdp)"
Subswitch of idump\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Selects print output regarding the interpolation matrices\&.

.PP
[4:4], iwdp >1 is only relevant for coupled systems\&. 0 No particular output\&. 1 Display table on interpolation matrices (full problem)\&. 2 In addition: same info for all submatrices\&. 3 In addition: connectivity info between unknowns\&. 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_levelx (int32_t levelx)"
Switch levelx
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
SAMG Safety limit: maximim number of levels to be created\&. Special debug option: Giving levelx a negative sign causes SAMG to display its convergence history also on coarser levels\&. Default = 25 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_napproach (int32_t napproach)"
Subswitch of nsolve\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
[1:1],napproach 1 Scalar approach (regardless of nsys) 2 Unknown-based (if used in scalar system napproach will be reset to 1) 3-5 Point32_t based approaches - selects type of interpolation to use 3: interp\&. is separate for each unknown 4: interp\&. is same " " " 5: interp\&. is point- (block-) wise 
.SS "void csmp::SAMG_Settings::Set_ncgrad (int32_t ncgrad)"
Subswitch of ncyc\&.
